Prefer Pointer Events when one interaction model should cover mouse, pen, and touch; inspect `pointerType` only when device-specific behavior is genuinely needed. Use `touch-action` to declare which browser panning or zooming behavior the region owns, rather than cancelling everything globally. For a draggable element, pointer capture keeps subsequent events targeted to that element even if the pointer leaves it. Handle `pointercancel` and release capture on completion or cancellation. Keep move handlers small and update only the visual state that must change. Preserve a keyboard and semantic alternative for actions that are not inherently spatial, because a gesture should not be the only way to complete a task.

```ts caption="Pointer capture keeps a drag associated with its target."
function startDrag(event: PointerEvent) {
  const target = event.currentTarget as HTMLElement;
  target.setPointerCapture(event.pointerId);
}

function endDrag(event: PointerEvent) {
  const target = event.currentTarget as HTMLElement;
  if (target.hasPointerCapture(event.pointerId)) {
    target.releasePointerCapture(event.pointerId);
  }
}
```
